course: technology
lesson: 32
title: Power stations and the grid
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Almost all the electricity in your house was made by something spinning. In a power station, steam, gas, wind or falling water spins a turbine, a wheel of blades, and the turbine turns a generator, where magnets whirl past coils of copper wire and push electricity along them.
explain: The power station can be far away, so tall steel pylons carry the electricity across the country on wires at very high voltage, which wastes less on the way. Near your town, transformers in a humming substation turn the voltage down so it is safe enough to use in homes.
explain: All the power stations and homes are joined in one big web called the grid, and people in a control room balance it every second. After a big football match, when millions of people put the kettle on at the same moment, they have to add power fast.
explain: Power lines and substations are for engineers only: never climb a pylon, keep kites and drones far from wires, and never go near a fallen line.
fact: In a power station, fuel or another source heats water into steam, which spins a turbine; the turbine turns a generator, where magnets and coils of wire make electricity.
fact: Almost all electricity, except from solar panels, comes from something spinning a generator.
fact: In a gas power station, burning gas spins a gas turbine like a jet engine, and the hot exhaust also makes steam for a second turbine; gas stations can start up quickly when demand jumps.
fact: In a nuclear power station, uranium atoms split and give off heat, which makes steam to spin turbines; a small pellet of uranium fuel holds about as much energy as a tonne of coal, and the sites are guarded and tightly controlled.
fact: The white cloud from a cooling tower is water vapour, not smoke; the towers are wide at the bottom and narrower in the middle, which helps air flow up through them.
fact: Pylons are tall steel towers that hold high-voltage wires high above the ground; electricity travels long distances at very high voltage because less is wasted that way.
fact: Never climb a pylon, and keep kites and drones far away from power lines.
fact: A transformer has two coils of wire round an iron core and changes the voltage, up for long lines and down for homes; substations hum because the iron vibrates.
fact: The national grid is the network of lines joining power stations and homes, and it is balanced every second; a sudden jump when millions of kettles go on after a big TV event is called a TV pick-up.
fact: In a power cut, use a torch, not a candle, keep the fridge closed, and never touch a fallen line; hospitals have generators that start automatically.
fact: A coal power station burns coal to make steam and gives off a lot of carbon dioxide and smoke; the UK closed its last one, Ratcliffe-on-Soar, in 2024.
fact: A hydroelectric dam lets water from a lake rush down through turbines; some stations pump water uphill at night and let it run down later, like a giant battery.
fact: Never swim near dams or reservoirs; the water is cold and deep, with hidden currents.
exercise: What does a generator need to make electricity in a power station? | Magnets spinning past coils of wire | Hot water poured onto metal plates | Light shining on silicon cells | Sparks jumping between two rods
exercise: What spins the turbine in most power stations? | Steam from heated water | Electricity from the grid | Sunlight falling on the blades | Workers turning a handle
exercise: Which way of making electricity does NOT spin a generator? | Solar panels | Wind turbines | Hydroelectric dams | Nuclear power stations
exercise: Why can a gas power station help when demand jumps suddenly? | It can start up quickly | It never needs any fuel | It stores electricity in its chimney | It runs only at night
exercise: What gives off the heat in a nuclear power station? | Uranium atoms splitting | Coal burning very slowly | Gas exploding in a tank | Lightning stored from storms
exercise: About how much coal holds as much energy as one small pellet of uranium fuel? | About a tonne | About a bucketful | About a spoonful | About one lump
exercise: What is the white cloud coming out of a cooling tower? | Water vapour | Smoke from burning coal | Poisonous gas | Dust from inside the tower
exercise: Why do pylons carry electricity at very high voltage? | Less electricity is wasted over long distances | High voltage makes the wires lighter | Birds cannot sit on high-voltage wires | Low voltage cannot go uphill
exercise: What does a transformer do? | Changes the voltage up or down | Makes new electricity from iron | Stores electricity for later | Turns electricity into gas
exercise: Why does a substation hum? | The iron in the transformers vibrates | Engineers play music to keep birds away | Electricity sings as it moves | Fans inside blow very hard
exercise: What is the national grid? | The network joining power stations to homes | A tall metal fence put up all the way round a power station | The main power station of the country | A map of all the pylons
exercise: What is a TV pick-up? | A jump in demand when millions of kettles go on after a TV event | A new TV channel about electricity | A van that collects old televisions | A power cut during a football match
exercise: What should you use for light in a power cut? | A torch | A candle | A gas stove | A match held up high
exercise: When did the UK close its last coal power station? | 2024 | 1958 | 1990 | 2050
exercise: How can a hydroelectric station work like a giant battery? | It pumps water uphill at night and lets it run down later | It fills its lake with batteries | It freezes water and melts it later | It keeps lightning in the dam
exercise: What does a hospital do when the power goes off? | Generators start up automatically | All the doctors go home | Patients pedal bikes to make power | It waits in the dark until power returns
test: «that cooling tower is pumping out tons of smoke, gross» What is the best correction? | The white cloud is water vapour, not smoke | You are right, it is coal smoke | It is steam mixed with bits of uranium from the reactor | It is a cloud made by the Moon
test: In a cartoon, a hero climbs a pylon to fix a wire himself. What is the real rule? | Never climb a pylon; only trained engineers work on lines | Climbing is fine as long as the hero wears thick rubber boots and gloves | Climbing is fine if the power is low that day | Climbing is fine during the day
test: What would happen if a country built power stations but no pylons or wires? | The electricity could not reach homes far away | Electricity would fly to homes through the air | Homes would get more electricity than before | Nothing, every home makes its own
test: The final whistle blows and millions of people switch on kettles at once. What does the grid control room do? | Adds power quickly from fast-starting stations | Switches off half the country | Nothing, kettles use no electricity | Asks everyone on the TV to switch their kettles off again
test: «storm knocked a wire down by the gate, it's just lying there, can i move it» What is the right answer? | Never touch it; keep away and tell an adult to call for help | Move it with a dry wooden stick, that's safe because wood doesn't carry electricity | Move it if you wear gloves | Move it if it is not sparking
test: «bloody power's out again, light a candle» What is the safer choice? | Use a torch, not a candle | Light several candles together | Light the gas cooker for light | Open the fridge to use its light
test: Count them: a line of pylons has one pylon every 300 metres. How many gaps are there between 5 pylons in a row? | 4 | 5 | 6 | 3
test: Count them: a power station has 3 turbines, and each one turns 1 generator. How many generators? | 3 | 1 | 6 | 9
test: «nuclear power just burns uranium like coal, right» What is the best answer? | No, uranium atoms split and give off heat, which makes steam | Yes, uranium burns with a big flame | No, uranium makes electricity directly with no steam | Yes, but uranium burns only at night
test: Why would a hydroelectric station pump water uphill at night when electricity is cheap? | To let it run down through turbines later when more is needed | To clean the water in the lake | To stop the lake freezing over on the coldest winter nights of the year | To make the dam heavier
test: Why do engineers send electricity at high voltage over long lines but lower it before it reaches homes? | High voltage wastes less on the way but is far too strong for homes | High voltage is cheaper to use in homes | Homes need more voltage than lines do | Low voltage travels faster on long lines
exam: Identify the principal means by which the majority of electricity is generated in a power station. | The rotation of a generator, in which magnets pass coils of wire, by means of a turbine | The heating of metal plates until they emit electricity | The collection of lightning in suitable containers | The direct conversion of coal into electricity without motion
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the white plume emitted from a cooling tower. | It consists of water vapour | It consists of smoke produced by combustion | It consists of radioactive gas | It consists of dust from the interior of the tower
exam: Ascertain the reason for which electricity is transmitted over long distances at a very high voltage. | The quantity of energy lost in transmission is thereby reduced | The weight of the conductors is thereby reduced | Birds are thereby prevented from alighting upon the conductors | Electricity of low voltage is incapable of ascending hills
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the conduct required of a person during an interruption of the electricity supply? | A torch is to be used in preference to a candle, and fallen lines are not to be touched | Candles are to be lit in every room | The refrigerator is to be opened frequently | Fallen lines are to be removed from the road
